`--order source` restores the pre-dependency-order behaviour: files are
emitted in the order they're passed, even when one references another.

  $ cat > z_base.ml <<EOF
  > [@@@css ".base{color:red;}"]
  > EOF

  $ cat > a_consumer.ml <<EOF
  > [@@@css ".consumer{color:blue;}"]
  > let _ = Z_base.x
  > EOF

  $ styled-ppx.generate --order source a_consumer.ml z_base.ml
  /* This file is generated by styled-ppx, do not edit manually */
  @layer styled-ppx.global, styled-ppx.descendant, styled-ppx.base, styled-ppx.conditional;
  @layer styled-ppx.global {
  .consumer{color:blue;}
  .base{color:red;}
  }

An unknown value is rejected up front, like the other flags.

  $ styled-ppx.generate --order sideways a_consumer.ml
  styled-ppx: invalid --order value "sideways" (expected "dependency" or "source")
  [2]

A trailing `--order` without its value is rejected too.

  $ styled-ppx.generate a_consumer.ml --order
  styled-ppx: missing value for flag "--order"
  [2]
